---
import StatusTag from '../components/StatusTag.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="MathGraph Protocol — Portable, inspectable evidence" description="An independently implementable protocol for typed claims, receipts, dependency resolution, immutable snapshots, and explicit temporal boundaries." currentPath="/protocol/">
  <header class="section section--compact">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">Protocol</div>
      <h1>Portable evidence without platform dependence.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Applications may depend on the protocol. The protocol does not depend on MathGraph.org, Metalogic-hosted services, LLMs, or Discover, Check, Resolve, or Develop services.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <strong>On this page</strong>
        <a href="#modules">Independent modules</a>
        <a href="#objects">Minimum P0 objects</a>
        <a href="#resolution">Resolution semantics</a>
        <a href="#identity">Identity and transport</a>
        <a href="#time">Temporal axes</a>
        <a href="#cryptography">Cryptography</a>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <section id="modules">
          <div class="eyebrow">Dependency direction</div>
          <h2>One protocol, separately usable functions</h2>
          <p>Independent producers can publish declared receipts and snapshots; independent consumers can validate and replay them without this website. The website is one static publisher and reader. It is not a required runtime, a certificate authority, or the source of the protocol’s validity.</p>
          <div class="maturity">
            <StatusTag label="Implemented" tone="implemented" />
            <div><strong>Check — Implemented at the qualified record-001 boundary</strong><p>The source-check record supports an exact record, version, source commit, suite commit, and goal match. It does not generalize beyond that tuple.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="maturity">
            <StatusTag label="Bounded-qualified" tone="bounded" />
            <div><strong>Resolve — Bounded-qualified offline for record 002</strong><p>The standard-library consumer replays one pinned historical dependency graph with a separately supplied trust-policy pin. The browser explorer only displays precomputed scenarios.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="maturity">
            <StatusTag label="Experimental" tone="experimental" />
            <div><strong>Discover — Experimental; no public protocol interface</strong><p>Research schedulers and residual discovery exist, but P0 publishes no Discover service or portable Discover contract.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="maturity">
            <StatusTag label="Experimental" tone="experimental" />
            <div><strong>Develop — Experimental; no public protocol interface</strong><p>Developmental research does not become a reusable capability merely because an experiment ran.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="maturity">
            <StatusTag label="Proposed" tone="proposed" />
            <div><strong>Capabilities — Proposed; no entries in P0</strong><p>No public capability registry or hosted capability interface is deployed.</p></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="objects">
          <div class="eyebrow">Minimum implemented substrate</div>
          <h2>Objects required by the bounded replay</h2>
          <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Implemented meaning in P0</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Typed claim</strong></td><td>An exact identity, kind, and scope. A changed kind or scope is a different request.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Evidence receipt</strong></td><td>A typed, scoped evidence identity admitted only under a declared verifier and trust policy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Dependency route</strong></td><td>A conclusion with explicit premise and evidence identities.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Resolution</strong></td><td>The deterministic before/after result computed from admitted roots and routes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Trust policy</strong></td><td>The separately pinned authority profile that admits exact source bytes, verifier identities, assumptions, and scopes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Immutable snapshot</strong></td><td>The byte-preserved historical claims, receipts, routes, withdrawals, and reported outcomes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Temporal/lifecycle axes</strong></td><td>Historical qualification, historical resolution, current applicability, freshness, revocation or supersession, lifecycle, and action authorization remain distinct.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <p>This is the minimum common model exercised by record 002, not a claim that every proposed MathGraph semantic object has a stable public contract.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="resolution">
          <h2>Rooted, exact-scope resolution</h2>
          <p>Each dependency route is conjunctive: every named premise and evidence receipt must be supported. Separate routes to the same conclusion are alternatives, so one route can disappear while another preserves support.</p>
          <p>Resolution computes a least fixed point from admitted qualified roots. A dependency cycle without such a root remains <code>UNKNOWN</code>; cycles cannot manufacture support or create circular self-justification.</p>
          <p>Claims, receipts, premises, verifier identities, assumptions, source pins, and scopes are exact. An unsupported semantic transport, implication, equivalence, kind change, or scope generalization returns <code>UNKNOWN</code> or an explicit validation error. Similar names, shapes, and hashes establish no correspondence.</p>
          <div class="callout callout--unknown"><StatusTag label="UNKNOWN" tone="unknown" /><p>Unknown is neither false nor malformed. It says the admitted evidence cannot warrant the requested interpretation.</p></div>
        </section>

        <section id="identity">
          <h2>Content identity is not claim truth</h2>
          <p>Canonical content identities make exact objects addressable. Source pins name repositories, commits, files, and toolchains. A content hash proves byte identity relative to an expected digest; it does not authenticate its own publisher or establish that the represented claim is correct.</p>
          <p>The historical Crystal/MGSO envelope remains an experimental semantic transport candidate. Its <code>MGSO</code> magic prefix and canonical encoding are not the deployed P0 record format, and unfamiliar object types must be preserved or declined—not coerced into known semantics.</p>
          <p>Verification receipts therefore keep native-verifier authority, source identity, assumptions, and result separate. External proof assistants and finite checkers retain their own semantics; MathGraph does not replace them.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="time">
          <h2>Historical qualification is not current applicability</h2>
          <p>An immutable snapshot can establish what qualified evidence supported at a pinned historical point. Historical qualification and historical support resolution remain distinct from current applicability, freshness, lifecycle, revocation or supersession, and action authorization.</p>
          <p>The offline record-002 replay reports current applicability as <code>UNKNOWN</code>, freshness as <code>NOT_CHECKED</code>, revocation or supersession as <code>NOT_CHECKED</code>, and action authorization as <code>NONE</code>. A withdrawal can make documentary support <code>STALE</code> without making a mathematical proposition <code>REFUTED</code>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="cryptography">
          <h2>Authenticity is not semantic correctness</h2>
          <p>A signature authenticates a signed statement under a key policy; it does not establish the truth of that statement. A cryptographic commitment likewise binds bytes without proving their mathematical or semantic meaning.</p>
          <div class="maturity">
            <StatusTag label="Experimental" tone="experimental" />
            <div><strong>Bounded ML-DSA-65 warrant experiment</strong><p>A finite Boolean witness protocol passed 23/23 research tests using ML-DSA-65 and SHA3-512 evidence binding. It has no independent security audit, implementation diversity, or production trust policy.</p></div>
          </div>
          <p>Interoperability with in-toto, Sigstore, SCITT, and RATS is under evaluation. None is an implemented MathGraph authority, conformance claim, partnership, or endorsement in P0.</p>
          <p>The experiment is evidence that a bounded warrant can be signed and replayed—not a claim of general security, universal verification, production post-quantum security, or semantic truth.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
